Now I see something very important! In the reference HLE:

```cpp
void another_world_cpu_device::nextThread()
{
	input_updatePlayer();  // Called BEFORE the thread increment!
	Thread* current;
	do {
		if (++m_currentThread == NUM_THREADS)
		{
			/* End of Frame */
			m_currentThread = 0;
			checkThreadRequests();
			((another_world_vm_state*) owner())->updateDisplay(0xFE);
		}
		current = &m_threads[m_currentThread];
	}
	while(current->state == FROZEN || current->PC == INACTIVE_THREAD);

	PC = current->PC;
}
```

The critical insight is that `input_updatePlayer()` happens at the start of `nextThread()` before any thread switching, the loop increments the thread counter first and wraps at frame boundaries, and only then does it check for frozen or inactive threads before loading the PC.

Now looking at our assembly implementation...

Implementing thread scheduling logic... So after the end-of-frame code sets the current thread back to 0, it does check thread 0 again before the loop condition—if thread 0 is active and not frozen, it'll process that thread in the same iteration rather than skipping ahead to thread 1.

Looking at our implementation, after loading A with 0 and falling through to the non-end-of-frame path, we check thread 0's state, and if it's active and unfrozen we exit the loop to process it; otherwise we increment A and scan forward through the remaining threads.

The key difference I'm noticing now is that the reference code calls `input_updatePlayer()` at the start of every thread switch in `nextThread()`, but our code appears to be handling input updates differently. I see the issue now—`input_updatePlayer()` runs unconditionally on every thread switch in the reference, writing to VM variables regardless of the current game part. The only conditional logic is checking for the Part 9 switch when bit 4 is pressed and we're not already in Part 0 or 9. My change to call it only at end-of-frame is a behavioral difference, though probably not the crash culprit. Let me examine the screen resource indexes more closely.

Looking at the array of screen resource IDs, the reference implementation handles a much broader set than mine does. I'm only covering 0x12, 0x13, 0x47, 0x49, and 0x53, but the full list includes 0x43, 0x44, 0x45, 0x46, 0x48, 0x90, and 0x91 as well—that's six resource IDs I'm not handling at all.

If the protection success path or intro sequence tries to load any of these missing screen resources, my code would route them to `_load_unknown` and silently skip them. The bytecode would then attempt to display a screen that was never actually loaded, resulting in garbage or black graphics on screen. The real issue is that `_load_screen_common` sets the CUR_VIDEO_2 pointer to the screen data before calling LOAD_SCREEN, so if that pointer is never initialized because the screen ID wasn't recognized, the display routine would be working with invalid data.

Looking deeper, if a screen ID isn't handled, CUR_VIDEO_2 stays unchanged. For Part 1, this is initialized to 0 since there's no video2 resource. If the bytecode then tries to load a missing screen like 0x44, my code ignores it and CUR_VIDEO_2 remains pointing to null. When the bytecode later executes a VIDEO 0x80 opcode that depends on CUR_VIDEO_2, it would dereference address 0 and crash the system.

The reference implementation handles this differently—it loads the screen data and updates CUR_VIDEO_2 properly. I'm missing several screen resource IDs: 0x43, 0x44, 0x45, 0x46, 0x48, 0x90, 0x91. The question is whether we actually have the resource data for these missing IDs in our resource table.

Looking at what we do have, I can see screen bitmaps for 0x12, 0x13, 0x47, 0x49, and 0x53, but nothing for those missing ones. Even if I added LOAD opcode handling for them, there's no data to load. Let me trace through what actually happens when the protection code wheel completes and transitions to the intro to see if any of these missing screens are even needed.

When Part 1 starts executing at PC=0, if it immediately tries to load a screen resource we don't have, we'd silently skip it and leave whatever was in the page buffer—possibly black after the part switch. That wouldn't crash though, unless subsequent VIDEO opcodes try to read from an uninitialized CUR_VIDEO_2 pointer.

But wait—initForPart resets CUR_VIDEO_2 to the part's video2 pointer (which is 0 for Part 1), so both the splash screen path and the code wheel path would end up with CUR_VIDEO_2 = 0. If the intro works fine coming from the splash screens, then CUR_VIDEO_2 being null probably isn't the culprit here.

Let me look at this from another angle. I should check if there's something in the input handling code that could be causing a crash during the part transition. Looking at the input update routine, I'm seeing how it queries the control panel segments to read direction and action buttons.

Now I'm tracing through what happens after the input routine returns—specifically, what register state we're left in and whether `_write_vm_var` is properly preserving or clobbering the values we need for the subsequent display update and tick tracking. After INPUT_UPDATE_PLAYER clobbers registers, CHECK_THREAD_REQUESTS is called. Let me check if CHECK_THREAD_REQUESTS properly initializes its registers...

It reads REQUESTED_NEXT_PART into WA, so any clobbering from INPUT_UPDATE_PLAYER is fine. But I need to verify whether INPUT_UPDATE_PLAYER clobbers XIX through its helper functions like `_cpanel_send_byte` and `_write_vm_var`. Looking at the call chain, XIX doesn't appear to be used by these functions, and tracing through NEXT_THREAD and its callers like PAUSE_THREAD, XIX isn't needed after INPUT_UPDATE_PLAYER returns, so the register state should be safe.

The user mentioned the bug might be in the protection screen success path, part-switching, or bitmap drawing for splash screens, so I should focus on those specific areas rather than register preservation.

The crash likely happens after switching to the intro part when it tries to display the game logo or another bitmap. Part 0's protection screen starts at 0x007B with splash screens like the Interplay logo, and after the code wheel succeeds, the bytecode probably sets a flag and either jumps to the splash screen code or requests Part 1. I need to trace what happens next in that transition. Maybe the code wheel screen never actually displays—the game could be jumping straight from the splash screens to the intro, skipping Part 0 entirely, which would explain why the user never sees the protection screen before the crash happens when they input the codes.

