Now I see something crucial! Look at `inte_w` (lines 284-292):

```cpp
void tmp94c241_device::inte_w(offs_t offset, uint8_t data)
{
    if (data & 0x80)
        data = (data & 0x7f) | (m_int_reg[offset] & 0x80);
    if (data & 0x08)
        data = (data & 0xf7) | (m_int_reg[offset] & 0x08);

    m_int_reg[offset] = data;
    m_check_irqs = 1;
}
```

This function preserves the pending flag bits (bit 7 and bit 3) when writing to an INTEx register — if those bits are set in the incoming data, it keeps whatever pending state was already there. And here's the key: `device_reset()` sets `m_int_reg[INTES1] |= 0x80`, meaning INTTX1 starts out pending from reset. So when `scNmod_w` gets called later, that pending flag is still there waiting to be processed.

Now I need to dig into how `check_irqs` actually evaluates whether an interrupt should fire, since the pending flag alone isn't enough — it has to pass a priority check.

